From 294072a4b6960ef4130caf72cfcc0401f76d49b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:55:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] fix(front): keep what an operator's argument names as
 TLC's SubscriptCollector reads it

TLC binds an operator's argument as a LazyValue of the argument in the
caller's context, and its SubscriptCollector reads a name through it,
by the node it names. A LET definition whose body is a literal TLC
holds as the literal's value (processConstants), which the collector
ignores; the front end bound an argument naming one to a copy of the
definition's body, which then read as a literal TLC can't analyze, so
`S(v) == x = 0 /\ [][x' = x]_<<x, v>>` with `Spec == LET L == 1 IN
S(L)` printed the warning that TLC can't tell whether the subscript
holds every variable, which TLC doesn't print. Such an argument is now
bound to the literal's value, through any number of operators.

TLC's lookup also gives an operator with no value of its own its
body's, and TLC gives every literal its value, so the collector
ignores an operator, a LET operator or a LAMBDA whose body is a
literal, where the front end entered the literal and printed the same
warning.
